          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size Against Starting Watts, Not Running Watts</h3>
          <p>
            Anything with a motor surges when it starts. A well pump drawing 1,200 running watts can demand 4,000 for a second or two, and a central air compressor is worse. Add the running watts of everything you want on at once, then confirm the generator's peak rating covers your largest motor's surge on top of that running total. Undersizing here does not show up on the spec sheet; it shows up as a tripped generator at 2 a.m.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Size Against the Fuel You Will Actually Use</h3>
          <p>
            Dual fuel machines lose roughly ten percent of their output on propane, and that loss lands squarely on surge capacity — precisely what starts the well pump. If propane is going to be your main fuel because it stores indefinitely, size against the propane rating rather than the headline gasoline figure. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">generator sizing guide</a> works through the arithmetic with real appliance figures, and the <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">wattage chart</a> lists starting and running watts by appliance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: Whole house -->
        <div id="whole-house" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Can a Portable Generator Run a Whole House?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Close enough to matter, with two honest caveats. A 10,000 to 12,000 watt portable connected through a transfer switch or an interlock kit will carry a refrigerator, a chest freezer, a furnace, a well pump, lighting across the house and — with care about what starts when — central air conditioning. For most households that is everything they actually miss during an outage.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first caveat is simultaneity. A standby generator sized for the house never asks you to think about which loads are on together. A portable at the top of its range does, particularly at the moment a compressor or a pump starts. In practice that means staging: get the heavy motors started one at a time rather than switching everything on at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is presence. A portable does not start itself. If the power fails while you are at work, on holiday or asleep, nothing happens until somebody goes outside and starts it — and the freezer has been thawing in the meantime. Households that are frequently away, or that depend on a sump pump to stay dry, are the clearest case for stepping up to a standby unit. The full comparison is in <a href="/portable-vs-standby-generator/">portable versus standby generators</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Smallest Generator That Can Run a House</h3>
          <p>
            If the question is the minimum rather than the maximum, the honest answer is around 5,000 running watts — enough to carry a refrigerator, a freezer, a furnace blower, a sump pump and lighting simultaneously, which keeps a house warm, dry and stocked. That is a genuinely liveable outage.
          </p>
          <p>
            Below roughly 3,500 running watts you are preserving food rather than running a house, which is a legitimate goal but a different one. And if central air or a well pump is non-negotiable, the real minimum jumps to 7,500 watts or more, because those two loads dominate everything else on the panel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
