                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The practical rule: if you need more than about 7,500 running watts, the decision is made for you and you are buying conventional. Below that, the question is whether you would rather have quiet or watts, and how close the neighbours are. A multi-day outage with an open-frame machine running twenty feet from a bedroom window is a genuinely unpleasant experience that no specification sheet conveys. Our <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">inverter generator testing</a> covers that side of the category in depth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What About Battery "Generators"?</h3>
          <p>
            Portable power stations are silent, produce no exhaust, and can run indoors — which no engine-driven generator can. The trade is capacity and recharging: they hold a fixed number of kilowatt-hours and then need mains power or solar to refill, so they suit short outages, apartments and medical equipment far better than a multi-day storm. Where they are the right answer, our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station picks</a> cover them, and <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">the direct comparison</a> sets out where each wins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <div id="connecting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Connecting It to the House
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Never Backfeed Through a Dryer Outlet</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Connecting a generator to household wiring with a double-male cord energises the utility lines outside your house and can kill the crew repairing the outage. It also bypasses every overcurrent device in your panel. It is illegal in most jurisdictions and it is the single most dangerous thing homeowners do with generators. Install a transfer switch or an interlock kit, or run extension cords directly to appliances. There is no third option.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            A transfer switch roughly doubles what a portable generator is worth to you, because extension cords can only reach things with plugs. The furnace, the well pump, the sump pump and the lighting circuits — the loads that actually determine whether a house is habitable — are hardwired, and only a transfer switch or interlock reaches them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Manual Transfer Switch</h3>
          <p>
            A small sub-panel beside your main panel carrying a fixed set of circuits, commonly six to ten, chosen at installation. During an outage you plug the generator into an exterior inlet box, start it, and flip each circuit from utility to generator. Typically $500 to $1,500 installed including the inlet, and it is the standard solution for a portable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interlock Kit</h3>
          <p>
            A mechanical plate on your existing panel that physically prevents the main breaker and a generator backfeed breaker being on at the same time. It gives access to every circuit rather than a fixed subset, usually for $200 to $500 installed. Availability depends on your panel brand and local code — the trade-offs are in <a href="/interlock-kit-vs-transfer-switch/">interlock kit versus transfer switch</a>, and installation costs in our <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch guide</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Extension Cords, Done Properly</h3>
          <p>
            If a switch is not in the budget yet, use heavy-gauge outdoor-rated cords — 12 gauge to 50 feet, 10 gauge beyond — run through a window with a sealed cord pass rather than a door left ajar, which defeats the whole setback. Never daisy-chain cords, and never run them through standing water or under rugs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fuel, Storage and Upkeep
          </h2>

          <p>
            The purchase price is the smaller half of owning a portable generator. The rest is fuel logistics and the discipline to keep a machine ready that spends fifty-one weeks a year doing nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 7,000 to 9,000 watt generator burns roughly half a gallon to a gallon of gasoline an hour at moderate load. Over a three-day outage that is 20 to 40 gallons — four to eight five-gallon cans, which is more than most households store and considerably more than they can buy after a regional storm, when the stations that still have power have hour-long queues and the ones that do not cannot pump at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is the strongest practical argument for a dual fuel machine. Propane stores indefinitely without stabiliser, cannot go stale, and sits in exchange cages outside every hardware store after the pumps have failed. Four 20-pound tanks in a garage corner represent days of essential power that needs no rotation. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-propane-does-a-generator-use/">propane consumption</a> works through how long each tank size actually lasts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exercise It Monthly</h3>
          <p>
            A carburetor gummed by stale gasoline is the number one reason a stored generator will not start when it is needed. Run the machine under load for 20 to 30 minutes every month. On a dual fuel unit, do those runs on propane, which leaves no varnish at all — this single habit prevents most no-start failures.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil Is the Real Consumable</h3>
          <p>
            These engines hold about a quart, so oil degrades far faster than in a car and a missed change matters more. Change after the first 20 to 25 hours, then every 50 to 100 hours of run time. During a long outage, check the level daily rather than trusting the interval — most units have a low-oil shutoff, and discovering that it works at 3 a.m. on day two is an avoidable problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Twenty Feet, Always</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Never run a generator in a garage, a breezeway, a covered porch, or within 20 feet of any window, door or vent — with the garage door open included, because that is not outdoors. Carbon monoxide from a portable generator can reach lethal concentrations in minutes, and CO shutoff sensors are a backup rather than a permission slip. Fit battery-powered CO detectors inside the house as a second layer.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two more practices matter in real use. Never refuel a hot or running engine — spilled gasoline on a hot exhaust is one of the most common causes of generator injury. And keep the machine dry and elevated in wet weather using a purpose-built generator canopy that preserves airflow and exhaust clearance, never a tarp draped over it, which traps exhaust heat against the housing. Whether to run one at all in bad weather is covered in <a href="/can-you-run-a-generator-in-the-rain/">running a generator in the rain</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

