          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best portable generator for home use in 2026?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Champion 9200-Watt Dual Fuel is our overall pick — enough output to carry essential circuits through a transfer switch, remote electric start, and propane capability for when the pumps are down. For near whole-house coverage including central air, the DuroMax XP12000EH; for quiet, the Westinghouse iGen4500; for value, the Westinghouse WGen9500DF.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable generator power a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A large one comes close. A 10,000 to 12,000 watt portable through a transfer switch will run a refrigerator, freezer, furnace, well pump, lighting and often central air — most of what a house actually uses in an outage. What it will not do is run everything at once without thought, or start itself when the power fails.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size portable generator do I need for home backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Essentials — fridge, freezer, lights, devices — need 3,500 to 4,500 running watts. Adding a sump pump and a window AC takes 5,500 to 7,000. Most essential circuits through a transfer switch need 8,000 to 9,500. A well pump plus central air needs 10,000 to 12,000.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the smallest generator that can run a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 5,000 running watts, on a selective definition of running a house — a refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, sump pump and lighting together, which keeps the place warm, dry and stocked. Below 3,500 you are preserving food rather than running a house, and central air or a well pump pushes the minimum to 7,500 or more.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you connect a portable generator to a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Through a manual transfer switch ($500-$1,500 installed) or an approved interlock kit ($200-$500), fitted by an electrician. Never backfeed through a dryer outlet with a double-male cord — it energises the utility line outside your home, can kill line crews, bypasses your panel's protection, and is illegal in most jurisdictions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can a portable generator run continuously?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most run 8 to 14 hours per tank at half load, but the limit is oil rather than fuel. Plan to shut down roughly every 24 hours to check and top up the oil. These engines hold about a quart, so oil degrades far faster than in a car, and a low-oil shutdown mid-outage is a self-inflicted problem.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are portable generators loud?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Open-frame conventional units run 70 to 76 dB at 23 feet under load — loud enough to be intrusive indoors and to strain neighbourly relations across a multi-day outage. Inverter generators run 48 to 58 dB because the engine varies its speed with demand rather than holding 3,600 rpm. That quiet costs roughly double per watt.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a portable generator better than a standby?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is cheaper by an order of magnitude and it requires you to be home. A portable runs $500 to $2,000 plus a transfer switch, and somebody must go out, start it and manage fuel. A standby is $5,000 to $15,000 installed, starts itself in seconds and runs off natural gas indefinitely. How often the house is empty usually decides it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes With Portable Generators
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It in a Garage With the Door Open</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An open garage is not outdoors, and carbon monoxide accumulates in it readily. This mistake kills people every winter. Twenty feet from any window, door or vent, exhaust pointed away from the house, with CO detectors inside as a second layer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding Through an Outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-male cord energises the utility lines outside your house and can kill the crew fixing the outage, while bypassing every breaker in your panel. Fit a transfer switch or an interlock kit, or run cords directly to appliances.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Watts You Cannot Move</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The step from 5,700 to 9,500 watts costs a few hundred dollars and about 80 pounds. If the machine lives in a shed at the end of the garden and needs to reach the inlet box, that weight decides whether it gets deployed at all — and whether it gets exercised monthly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing It With Untreated Gasoline</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gummed carburetor is the leading reason a stored generator will not start during an outage. Either stabilise the fuel and run it through the carburetor, or shut the fuel valve and let the bowl run dry — and exercise the unit monthly regardless.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Starting Watts When Sizing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A well pump running at 1,200 watts can demand 4,000 to start. Size against the surge of your largest motor stacked on top of everything already running, or the generator will trip at the worst possible moment and you will blame the machine rather than the arithmetic.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

