          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The <strong class="text-white">best portable generator</strong> for most homes is the <strong class="text-white">Champion 9200-Watt Dual Fuel</strong> — enough output to carry the circuits that matter through a transfer switch, remote electric start so it actually gets used in bad weather, and propane capability for when the pumps are down. For near whole-house coverage including central air, the <strong class="text-white">DuroMax XP12000EH</strong>; for quiet, the <strong class="text-white">Westinghouse iGen4500</strong>; for value, the <strong class="text-white">WGen9500DF</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Size against <strong class="text-white">starting watts</strong>, not running watts, and against the fuel you will genuinely use. Around 5,000 running watts is the realistic minimum to run a house selectively; 10,000 or more gets you close to whole-panel coverage including air conditioning.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then spend the money that makes it work: a <strong class="text-white">transfer switch or interlock kit</strong>, so the generator reaches the hardwired circuits that actually matter, and a monthly exercise run so it starts when the lights go out. Never backfeed, and never run it within twenty feet of a window.
